{{Elections in South Carolina}}

The '''2022 United States Senate election in South Carolina''' was held on November 8, 2022, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent the [[U.S. state|state]] of [[South Carolina]]. Incumbent Republican Senator [[Tim Scott]] won reelection to a second full term, defeating [[South Carolina House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[Krystle Matthews]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 8, 2022 |title=2022 US Senate Election Results: Live Map |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Elections/2022-us-senate-election-results-live-map/ |access-date=2022-11-09 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> This was the third consecutive election for this seat in which both major party nominees were Black.

Scott was appointed to the U.S. Senate in 2013 following the resignation of fellow Republican [[Jim DeMint]]. With 61.1% of the vote, he won the [[2014 United States Senate special election in South Carolina|2014 special election]] to serve the remainder of DeMint's term. Scott was then re-elected to a full six-year term in [[2016 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2016]] with 60.6% of the vote.

Primary elections in South Carolina were held on June 14, 2022. Scott won the Republican primary unopposed, while Matthews won the Democratic primary in a June 28 runoff against [[historic preservationist]] [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=News 4 |first=A. B. C. |date=2022-06-28 |title=Krystle Matthews wins primary runoff despite controversial leaked call with inmate |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/krystle-matthew-wins-primary-runoff-despite-controversial-leaked-call-with-inmate |access-date=2022-09-30 |website=WCIV |language=en}}</ref>

